IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Crimes Act 1914 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the person arrested for, charged with or convicted of a Commonwealth
-- offence meets: arrest without warrant and being told why (ss 3W, 3ZC, 3ZD); Part IC -- who is "under arrest" or a
-- "protected suspect" (s 23B), how long I can be held for investigation (s 23C), an extension by a magistrate (ss 23D, 23DA),
-- the caution (s 23F), my right to contact a friend, relative and lawyer (s 23G) and its exceptions (s 23L), the interview
-- friend for a child or an Aboriginal person or Torres Strait Islander (ss 23H, 23K), an interpreter (s 23N), my consulate
-- (s 23P), humane treatment (s 23Q), my right to silence (s 23S), recording of my admissions (s 23V); Part IB sentencing --
-- what the court must weigh (s 16A), fines (s 16C), the last-resort rule for prison (ss 17A, 17B), dismissal or discharge
-- without conviction (s 19B), release on a recognizance (s 20); and Part VIIC -- when my conviction is spent and whether I
-- must disclose it (ss 85ZL, 85ZM, 85ZV, 85ZW, 85ZX, 85ZY, Division 6).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- "I have been arrested. How long can they hold me? Do I have to answer? Can I call a lawyer? If
-- I am convicted, will I go to prison, can I avoid a conviction? When can I stop telling employers about it?" Provisions
-- addressed to investigating officials, magistrates or courts are encoded as what they mean for that person: what must be done
-- for me, by when, and what the court may or must do with me. Every belief, satisfaction or opinion of an official or court is
-- asked as an input, never guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2026C00368 (Compilation No. 167, compilation date 27 August 2026; Federal Register
-- of Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C1914A00012.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part IAA beyond ss 3W, 3ZC, 3ZD (search warrants, frisk and ordinary searches, terrorism and airport powers,
-- age determination, information-gathering); Parts IAAA-IAC (delayed notification warrants, monitoring, account takeover,
-- controlled operations, integrity testing, assumed identities, witness protection); Part IC's terrorism arm (ss 3WA, 23DB-
-- 23DF: a terrorism arrest answers `not covered`); Part ID (forensic procedures); Part IB beyond ss 16A, 16C, 17A, 17B, 19B,
-- 20 (non-parole periods, recognizance release orders, parole, mental impairment, minimum penalties); the offences in Parts II
-- to VIIB; Part VIIC Divisions 2 (pardons, quashed convictions) and 5 (complaints). The value of a penalty unit is not
-- computed (s 4AA's indexation formula is missing from the deposit -- NOTES.md finding 14). A State offence asked about in a
-- State by anyone other than a Commonwealth authority answers `not covered`.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the accused's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Crimes Act 1914 -- the person arrested, charged or convicted`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- ss 3WA, 23DB-23DF
    `I was arrested for a terrorism offence: the terrorism arrest power and investigation period (ss 3WA, 23DB to 23DF) are not encoded`
    -- s 85ZV(2), (3)
    `my conviction is for a State offence and the person asking is not a Commonwealth authority in a State: the State's own spent convictions law decides it (s 85ZV(2), (3)), and that law is not encoded here`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `ss 3W, 3ZC, 3ZD -- if a police officer wants to arrest you`

-- s 3W(1): "A constable may, without warrant, arrest a person for an offence (other than a terrorism offence and an offence
-- against section 80.2C of the Criminal Code) if the constable believes on reasonable grounds that: (a) the person has
-- committed or is committing the offence; and (b) proceedings by summons against the person would not achieve one or more of
-- the following purposes": (i) my appearance in court, (ii) preventing repetition or another offence, (iii) preventing loss
-- of evidence, (iv) preventing harassment of witnesses, (v) preventing fabrication of evidence, (vi) my own safety or welfare.
-- (2): if, "before the person is charged ..., the constable in charge of the investigation ceases to believe on reasonable
-- grounds" either thing, "the person must be released." s 3ZD(1): "must inform the other person, at the time of the arrest,
-- of the offence"; (2) the substance is enough; (3) not if I "should, in the circumstances, know the substance" or my actions
-- make it impracticable. s 3ZC(1): no "more force, or ... greater indignity, than is necessary and reasonable".
DECLARE `Can I be arrested without a warrant` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the officer may arrest me without a warrant, and must tell me the substance of the offence at the time of arrest unless I should already know it or my actions make that impracticable (ss 3W(1), 3ZD), using no more force or indignity than is necessary and reasonable (s 3ZC)`
    `no: the officer does not believe on reasonable grounds that I committed the offence, so s 3W(1) gives no power to arrest me without a warrant`
    `no: proceeding by summons would achieve every s 3W(1)(b) purpose, so s 3W(1) gives no power to arrest me without a warrant; I should be summonsed instead`
    `I must be released: I was arrested under s 3W(1) but, before being charged, the officer in charge no longer believes on reasonable grounds that I committed the offence or that holding me is necessary (s 3W(2))`

@export Can a police officer arrest me without a warrant for a Commonwealth offence?
GIVEN `it is a terrorism or s 80.2C offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is a terrorism offence or an offence of advocating terrorism (Criminal Code s 80.2C)
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer believes on reasonable grounds that you have committed, or are committing, the offence
      `the officer believes a summons would not do` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer believes on reasonable grounds that a summons would not ensure you come to court, stop the offence continuing or another being committed, protect evidence or witnesses, prevent evidence being fabricated, or keep you safe
      `I have already been arrested and not yet charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already been arrested for the offence under this power and have not yet been charged
      `the officer in charge has stopped believing it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before you were charged, the officer in charge of the investigation stopped believing on reasonable grounds that you committed the offence, or that holding you in custody is necessary for one of those purposes
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I be arrested without a warrant`
`Can I be arrested without a warrant question` MEANS
    IF `it is a terrorism or s 80.2C offence`
    THEN `not covered` `I was arrested for a terrorism offence: the terrorism arrest power and investigation period (ss 3WA, 23DB to 23DF) are not encoded`
    ELSE IF `I have already been arrested and not yet charged` AND `the officer in charge has stopped believing it`
         THEN `answered` `I must be released: I was arrested under s 3W(1) but, before being charged, the officer in charge no longer believes on reasonable grounds that I committed the offence or that holding me is necessary (s 3W(2))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the officer believes on reasonable grounds I committed it`)
              THEN `answered` `no: the officer does not believe on reasonable grounds that I committed the offence, so s 3W(1) gives no power to arrest me without a warrant`
              ELSE IF (NOT `the officer believes a summons would not do`)
                   THEN `answered` `no: proceeding by summons would achieve every s 3W(1)(b) purpose, so s 3W(1) gives no power to arrest me without a warrant; I should be summonsed instead`
                   ELSE `answered` `yes: the officer may arrest me without a warrant, and must tell me the substance of the offence at the time of arrest unless I should already know it or my actions make that impracticable (ss 3W(1), 3ZD), using no more force or indignity than is necessary and reasonable (s 3ZC)`

§§ `s 23B -- am I "under arrest" or a "protected suspect" (if police are questioning you)`

-- s 23B(1): "under arrest: a person is under arrest if: (a) the person is arrested for a Commonwealth offence; and (b) the
-- person's arrest has not ceased under subsection (3) or (4); and (c) the person has not been released." "Commonwealth
-- offence means: (a) an offence against a law of the Commonwealth, other than ... a service offence ...; or (b) a State
-- offence that has a federal aspect." (3): arrest ceases on remand by a bail authority. (2): "A person is a protected suspect
-- if: (a) the person is in the company of an investigating official for the purpose of being questioned about a Commonwealth
-- offence; and (b) the person has not been arrested for the offence; and (c) one or more of the following applies ...: (i)
-- the official believes that there is sufficient evidence to establish that the person has committed the offence; (ii) the
-- official would not allow the person to leave ...; (iii) the official has given the person reasonable grounds for believing
-- that the person would not be allowed to leave ...; and (d) none of the following applies": border functions without a
-- belief that I committed a Commonwealth offence, or a power "to detain and search the person" or "to require the person to
-- provide information or to answer questions"; "(e) the person has not ceased to be a suspect under subsection (4)" (taking
-- part voluntarily in covert investigations of someone else -- asked within (a)).
-- Division 3 (ss 23F-23V) protects both.
DECLARE `My standing under Part IC` IS ONE OF
    `under arrest: Part IC Division 2 (detention for investigation) and Division 3 (the caution, contact with a friend, relative and lawyer, and the rest) apply to me`
    `a protected suspect: I am not under arrest, but Part IC Division 3 (the caution, contact with a friend, relative and lawyer, and the rest) applies to me; Division 2 detention does not`
    `neither: Part IC's protections for people under arrest and protected suspects do not apply to me`

GIVEN `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official would not let me leave` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My standing under Part IC`
`my standing under Part IC` MEANS
    IF `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded`
    THEN `under arrest: Part IC Division 2 (detention for investigation) and Division 3 (the caution, contact with a friend, relative and lawyer, and the rest) apply to me`
    ELSE IF     `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence`
                 AND (`the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` OR `the official would not let me leave` OR `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave`)
                 AND (NOT `the official is exercising a border, search or information power`)
         THEN `a protected suspect: I am not under arrest, but Part IC Division 3 (the caution, contact with a friend, relative and lawyer, and the rest) applies to me; Division 2 detention does not`
         ELSE `neither: Part IC's protections for people under arrest and protected suspects do not apply to me`

@export The police want to question me about a Commonwealth offence. Am I "under arrest" or a "protected suspect", so that Part IC protects me?
GIVEN `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence (or a State offence with a federal aspect) and have not been released or remanded by a bail authority
      `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are with a police officer (or another official with power to arrest for Commonwealth offences) so that they can question you about a Commonwealth offence, other than as a voluntary participant in a covert investigation of someone else
      `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes there is sufficient evidence to establish that you committed the offence
      `the official would not let me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official would not let you leave if you wished to
      `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official has given you reasonable grounds for believing you would not be allowed to leave if you wished to
      `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official is dealing with people or goods entering or leaving Australia and does not believe you committed a Commonwealth offence, or is using a Commonwealth power to detain and search you or to require you to give information or answer questions
GIVETH A `My standing under Part IC`
`Am I under arrest or a protected suspect` MEANS
    `my standing under Part IC` `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` `the official would not let me leave` `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` `the official is exercising a border, search or information power`

§§ `ss 23C, 23D, 23DA -- how long I can be held for investigation (if you have been arrested)`

-- s 23C(2): while arrested I "may ... be detained for the purpose of investigating" whether I committed the offence or another
-- Commonwealth offence an investigating official reasonably suspects. (4): "the investigation period begins when the person is
-- arrested, and ends at a time thereafter that is reasonable, having regard to all the circumstances, but does not extend
-- beyond: (a) if the person is or appears to be under 18, an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander--2 hours; or (b) in
-- any other case--4 hours; after the arrest, unless the period is extended under section 23DA." (6): if I was "arrested more
-- than once within any period of 48 hours", the period for each later arrest "is reduced by so much of any ... earlier
-- investigation period ... as occurred within that 48 hours" (subject to (6A) -- asked as the hours to subtract). (7): "disregard
-- any reasonable time during which the questioning of the person is suspended, or delayed" for listed reasons (taking me to a
-- station, contacting a lawyer, waiting for them, medical attention, intoxication, an identification parade, rest, and others);
-- (7A) not if I am questioned in that time. (8): the prosecution bears the burden of proving time was disregardable.
-- s 23DA(7): an extension "for a period not exceeding 8 hours, and must not be extended more than once", only for a "serious
-- Commonwealth offence" -- "punishable by imprisonment for a period exceeding 12 months" (s 23B(1)) -- and only if the
-- magistrate is satisfied of the s 23DA(2) matters.
-- The answer is the latest the investigation period can run, in hours after the arrest, with disregarded time added back. The
-- period may end sooner: it ends at "a time ... that is reasonable", which is the court's to judge.
GIVEN `the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` IS A NUMBER
      `the hours of extension a magistrate granted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the hours of extension that count` MEANS
    IF `the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` GREATER THAN 12
    THEN IF `the hours of extension a magistrate granted` GREATER THAN 8
         THEN 8
         ELSE `the hours of extension a magistrate granted`
    ELSE 0

@export I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence. At most how many hours after my arrest can the police keep investigating before they must release me or take me to a bail authority?
GIVEN `it is a terrorism offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were arrested for a terrorism offence
      `I am, or appear to be, under 18, Aboriginal or a Torres Strait Islander` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are, or appear to be, under 18 years old, an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander
      `the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum term of imprisonment, in months, for the offence you were arrested for (0 if it is not punishable by imprisonment)
      `the hours of extension a magistrate granted` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours of extension a magistrate granted under s 23DA (0 if none)
      `hours of earlier investigation in the last 48 hours` IS A NUMBER @desc If you were arrested more than once in a 48-hour period, the hours of earlier investigation periods within those 48 hours that count against this one (0 if this is your only arrest)
      `hours of questioning suspended for allowed reasons` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours during which questioning was reasonably suspended or delayed for a reason s 23C(7) allows (for example taking you to the station, letting you call or wait for a lawyer, medical attention, rest) and you were not questioned
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`The latest hour my investigation period can end` MEANS
    IF `it is a terrorism offence`
    THEN `not covered` `I was arrested for a terrorism offence: the terrorism arrest power and investigation period (ss 3WA, 23DB to 23DF) are not encoded`
    ELSE `answered` (`the base hours` MINUS `hours of earlier investigation in the last 48 hours` PLUS (`the hours of extension that count` `the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` `the hours of extension a magistrate granted`) PLUS `hours of questioning suspended for allowed reasons`)
    WHERE
        `the base hours` MEANS
            IF `I am, or appear to be, under 18, Aboriginal or a Torres Strait Islander`
            THEN 2
            ELSE 4

-- s 23C(3): "If the person is not released within the investigation period, the person must be brought before a bail
-- authority within the investigation period or, if it is not practicable to do so within the investigation period, as soon as
-- practicable after the end of the investigation period." (2A): the s 23C detention power ends with the period. (8)(a): the
-- prosecution bears the burden of proving "as soon as practicable".
DECLARE `What must happen at the end of my investigation period` IS ONE OF
    `nothing more under s 23C: I have been released`
    `I must be brought before a bail authority (a magistrate, justice or other bail officer) within the investigation period, or if that is not practicable, as soon as practicable after it ends; the power to hold me for investigation ends with the period (s 23C(2A), (3))`

@export My investigation period is running out. What must happen to me?
GIVEN `I have been released` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the police have released you (with or without charge, or on police bail)
GIVETH A `What must happen at the end of my investigation period`
`What must happen at the end of my investigation period question` MEANS
    IF `I have been released`
    THEN `nothing more under s 23C: I have been released`
    ELSE `I must be brought before a bail authority (a magistrate, justice or other bail officer) within the investigation period, or if that is not practicable, as soon as practicable after it ends; the power to hold me for investigation ends with the period (s 23C(2A), (3))`

-- s 23D(1): only for "a serious Commonwealth offence (other than a terrorism offence)", applied for "at or before the end of
-- the investigation period". (5): before the magistrate considers it, the official "must" give me or my lawyer a copy (or tell
-- us its contents) and "must inform the person that he or she, or his or her legal representative, may make representations".
-- s 23DA(2): the magistrate may extend if satisfied "(a) the offence is a serious Commonwealth offence ...; (b) further detention
-- ... is necessary to preserve or obtain evidence or to complete the investigation ...; (c) the investigation ... is being
-- conducted properly and without delay; and (d) the person, or his or her legal representative, has been given the opportunity
-- to make representations". (6): I get a copy of the instrument. (7): not exceeding 8 hours, and only once.
DECLARE `Can my investigation period be extended` IS ONE OF
    `no: an extension is possible only for a serious Commonwealth offence, one punishable by more than 12 months' imprisonment (ss 23B(1), 23D(1))`
    `no: the period has already been extended once, and it must not be extended more than once (s 23DA(7))`
    `no: the magistrate is not satisfied that further detention is necessary, that the investigation is proper and without delay, and that I have had the chance to make representations (s 23DA(2))`
    `yes, by up to 8 hours: the magistrate may extend it once; I or my lawyer must first be given the application (or told what it says) and told we may make representations, and must be given a copy of the extension (ss 23D(5), 23DA(6), (7))`

@export The police have applied to a magistrate to hold me longer. Can my investigation period be extended?
GIVEN `the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum term of imprisonment, in months, for the offence you were arrested for (0 if it is not punishable by imprisonment)
      `it has already been extended once` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a magistrate has already extended this investigation period
      `the magistrate is satisfied of the s 23DA(2) matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the magistrate is satisfied that further detention is necessary to preserve or obtain evidence or complete the investigation, that the investigation is being conducted properly and without delay, and that you or your lawyer had the chance to make representations
GIVETH A `Can my investigation period be extended`
`Can my investigation period be extended question` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the offence's maximum imprisonment in months` GREATER THAN 12))
    THEN `no: an extension is possible only for a serious Commonwealth offence, one punishable by more than 12 months' imprisonment (ss 23B(1), 23D(1))`
    ELSE IF `it has already been extended once`
         THEN `no: the period has already been extended once, and it must not be extended more than once (s 23DA(7))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the magistrate is satisfied of the s 23DA(2) matters`)
              THEN `no: the magistrate is not satisfied that further detention is necessary, that the investigation is proper and without delay, and that I have had the chance to make representations (s 23DA(2))`
              ELSE `yes, by up to 8 hours: the magistrate may extend it once; I or my lawyer must first be given the application (or told what it says) and told we may make representations, and must be given a copy of the extension (ss 23D(5), 23DA(6), (7))`

§§ `ss 23F, 23S -- do I have to answer, and must I be cautioned (if police are questioning you)`

-- s 23F(1): "if a person is under arrest or a protected suspect, an investigating official must, before starting to question
-- the person, caution the person that he or she does not have to say or do anything, but that anything the person does say or
-- do may be used in evidence." (3): not "so far as another law of the Commonwealth requires the person to answer questions".
-- s 23S: "Nothing in this Part affects: (a) the right of a person to refuse to answer questions or to participate in an
-- investigation except where required to do so by or under an Act". (The consequence of a missing caution for the evidence is
-- in the Evidence Act 1995, s 139 -- see that baseline.)
DECLARE `Must I be cautioned` IS ONE OF
    `yes: before questioning starts I must be cautioned that I do not have to say or do anything, but that anything I say or do may be used in evidence, in a language I can communicate in with reasonable fluency, and the caution and my responses must be recorded if practicable (ss 23B(1) "inform", 23F, 23U)`
    `no caution is required: another Commonwealth law requires me to answer these questions or do these things (s 23F(3))`
    `Part IC requires no caution: I am neither under arrest nor a protected suspect`

@export Must the police caution me before questioning me?
GIVEN `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and have not been released or remanded by a bail authority
      `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are with a police officer (or another official with power to arrest for Commonwealth offences) so that they can question you about a Commonwealth offence
      `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes there is sufficient evidence to establish that you committed the offence
      `the official would not let me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official would not let you leave if you wished to
      `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official has given you reasonable grounds for believing you would not be allowed to leave
      `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official is dealing with people or goods entering or leaving Australia and does not believe you committed an offence, or is using a power to detain and search you or to require you to give information or answer questions
      `another Commonwealth law requires me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Commonwealth law requires you to answer these questions or do the things asked
GIVETH A `Must I be cautioned`
`Must I be cautioned question` MEANS
    IF `my standing under Part IC` `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` `the official would not let me leave` `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` EQUALS `neither: Part IC's protections for people under arrest and protected suspects do not apply to me`
    THEN `Part IC requires no caution: I am neither under arrest nor a protected suspect`
    ELSE IF `another Commonwealth law requires me to answer`
         THEN `no caution is required: another Commonwealth law requires me to answer these questions or do these things (s 23F(3))`
         ELSE `yes: before questioning starts I must be cautioned that I do not have to say or do anything, but that anything I say or do may be used in evidence, in a language I can communicate in with reasonable fluency, and the caution and my responses must be recorded if practicable (ss 23B(1) "inform", 23F, 23U)`

@export Do I have to answer the police's questions?
GIVEN `an Act requires me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act, or something done under an Act, requires you to answer these questions or take part in the investigation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have to answer police questions` IF
    `an Act requires me to answer`

§§ `ss 23G, 23L -- calling a friend, relative or lawyer (if you have been arrested or are a protected suspect)`

-- s 23G(1): "Subject to section 23L, ... an investigating official must, before starting to question the person, inform the
-- person that he or she may: (a) communicate, or attempt to communicate, with a friend or relative to inform that person of his
-- or her whereabouts; and (b) communicate ... with a legal practitioner of the person's choice and arrange ... for a legal
-- practitioner of the person's choice to be present during the questioning; and ... must defer the questioning for a reasonable
-- time". (2): "reasonable facilities", and a lawyer call not overheard "as far as practicable". (3): private consultation; the
-- lawyer may be present and advise, "but only while the legal practitioner does not unreasonably interfere".
-- s 23L(1): the requirement does not apply "if, and for so long as, the official believes on reasonable grounds that:
-- (a) compliance ... is likely to result in: (i) an accomplice of the person taking steps to avoid apprehension; or (ii) the
-- concealment, fabrication or destruction of evidence or the intimidation of a witness; or (b) ... the questioning is so urgent,
-- having regard to the safety of other people, that it should not be delayed". (2): for the lawyer, only "in exceptional
-- circumstances" and with the written authority of a Superintendent or higher. (3): comply "as soon as possible" after. (4): if
-- I am kept from my chosen lawyer, "the investigating official must offer the services of another legal practitioner".
DECLARE `May I contact a lawyer before questioning` IS ONE OF
    `yes: before questioning I must be told I may contact a friend or relative and a lawyer of my choice, have the lawyer present, and consult them in private; questioning must wait a reasonable time for that (s 23G)`
    `not my chosen lawyer for now: an exception has been authorised for exceptional circumstances (s 23L(1), (2)); I must be offered another lawyer, and my rights return as soon as the exception ends (s 23L(3), (4))`
    `the lawyer yes, the friend or relative not for now: the official believes on reasonable grounds that contacting them risks an accomplice escaping, evidence being lost or a witness intimidated, or that questioning is urgent for others' safety (s 23L(1)); I must still be told I may contact a lawyer (s 23G(1)(b))`
    `Part IC gives me no such right: I am neither under arrest nor a protected suspect`

@export Can I call a lawyer, friend or relative before the police question me?
GIVEN `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and have not been released or remanded by a bail authority
      `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are with a police officer (or another official with power to arrest for Commonwealth offences) so that they can question you about a Commonwealth offence
      `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes there is sufficient evidence to establish that you committed the offence
      `the official would not let me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official would not let you leave if you wished to
      `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official has given you reasonable grounds for believing you would not be allowed to leave
      `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official is dealing with people or goods entering or leaving Australia and does not believe you committed an offence, or is using a power to detain and search you or to require you to give information or answer questions
      `the official believes contact risks evidence, witnesses, an accomplice or safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes on reasonable grounds that letting you make contact is likely to let an accomplice escape, or evidence be concealed, fabricated or destroyed, or a witness be intimidated, or that questioning is so urgent for others' safety that it cannot wait
      `a superintendent has authorised keeping my lawyer away for exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in exceptional circumstances, a police officer of the rank of Superintendent or higher has authorised the exception for your lawyer and recorded the official's grounds
GIVETH A `May I contact a lawyer before questioning`
`May I contact a lawyer before questioning question` MEANS
    IF `my standing under Part IC` `I have been arrested for a Commonwealth offence and not released or remanded` `I am with an investigator to be questioned about a Commonwealth offence` `the official believes there is enough evidence I committed it` `the official would not let me leave` `the official has given me reasonable grounds to think I could not leave` `the official is exercising a border, search or information power` EQUALS `neither: Part IC's protections for people under arrest and protected suspects do not apply to me`
    THEN `Part IC gives me no such right: I am neither under arrest nor a protected suspect`
    ELSE IF `the official believes contact risks evidence, witnesses, an accomplice or safety` AND `a superintendent has authorised keeping my lawyer away for exceptional circumstances`
         THEN `not my chosen lawyer for now: an exception has been authorised for exceptional circumstances (s 23L(1), (2)); I must be offered another lawyer, and my rights return as soon as the exception ends (s 23L(3), (4))`
         ELSE IF `the official believes contact risks evidence, witnesses, an accomplice or safety`
              THEN `the lawyer yes, the friend or relative not for now: the official believes on reasonable grounds that contacting them risks an accomplice escaping, evidence being lost or a witness intimidated, or that questioning is urgent for others' safety (s 23L(1)); I must still be told I may contact a lawyer (s 23G(1)(b))`
              ELSE `yes: before questioning I must be told I may contact a friend or relative and a lawyer of my choice, have the lawyer present, and consult them in private; questioning must wait a reasonable time for that (s 23G)`

§§ `ss 23H, 23K, 23N, 23P -- if you are under 18, Aboriginal or a Torres Strait Islander, need an interpreter, or are not an Australian citizen`

-- s 23K(1): if the official "believes on reasonable grounds that the person is under 18", the official "must not question the
-- person unless an interview friend is present ... and, before the start of the questioning, the official has allowed the
-- person to communicate with the interview friend" privately (subject to s 23L). (3): a parent or guardian or lawyer; failing
-- them a relative or friend acceptable to me; failing them, for an Aboriginal person or Torres Strait Islander, a
-- representative of an Aboriginal legal assistance organisation; failing all, "an independent person". No waiver.
-- s 23H(2): for an Aboriginal person or Torres Strait Islander (believed on reasonable grounds), no questioning unless "(c) an
-- interview friend is present ... or (d) the person has expressly and voluntarily waived his or her right"; (4) the prosecution
-- must prove the waiver, made "with full knowledge and understanding". (7): if under 18, s 23K applies instead. (8): not
-- required if the official "believes on reasonable grounds that, having regard to the person's level of education and
-- understanding, the person is not at a disadvantage ... in comparison with members of the Australian community generally".
-- (1), (1AB): unless I have arranged a lawyer, an Aboriginal legal assistance organisation must be notified and questioning wait
-- until its representative has communicated with me or 2 hours have passed.
DECLARE `Must I have an interview friend` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am believed to be under 18, so I must not be questioned unless an interview friend (a parent, guardian or lawyer; failing them a relative or friend I accept; failing them an independent person) is present and I have first been able to speak with them privately; I cannot waive this (s 23K)`
    `yes, unless I waive it: I am believed to be an Aboriginal person or Torres Strait Islander, so I must not be questioned unless an interview friend I choose (or, failing that, an Aboriginal legal assistance representative) is present and I have spoken with them privately; and unless I have arranged a lawyer, an Aboriginal legal assistance organisation must be notified first (s 23H)`
    `no: I have expressly and voluntarily waived my right to an interview friend; the prosecution must prove I did so with full knowledge and understanding (s 23H(2)(d), (4))`
    `no: the official believes on reasonable grounds that, given my education and understanding, I am not at a disadvantage in the questioning compared with the Australian community generally (s 23H(8))`
    `no: neither s 23K nor s 23H applies to me`

@export Must the police have an interview friend with me before they question me?
GIVEN `the official believes I am under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes on reasonable grounds that you are under 18 (and you are being interviewed as a suspect, or are under arrest or a protected suspect)
      `the official believes I am Aboriginal or a Torres Strait Islander` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes on reasonable grounds that you are an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander (and you are being interviewed as a suspect, or are under arrest or a protected suspect)
      `I have expressly and voluntarily waived an interview friend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have expressly and voluntarily given up your right to have an interview friend present
      `the official believes I am at no disadvantage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes on reasonable grounds that, given your level of education and understanding, you are not at a disadvantage in the questioning compared with members of the Australian community generally
GIVETH A `Must I have an interview friend`
`Must I have an interview friend question` MEANS
    IF `the official believes I am under 18`
    THEN `yes: I am believed to be under 18, so I must not be questioned unless an interview friend (a parent, guardian or lawyer; failing them a relative or friend I accept; failing them an independent person) is present and I have first been able to speak with them privately; I cannot waive this (s 23K)`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the official believes I am Aboriginal or a Torres Strait Islander`)
         THEN `no: neither s 23K nor s 23H applies to me`
         ELSE IF `the official believes I am at no disadvantage`
              THEN `no: the official believes on reasonable grounds that, given my education and understanding, I am not at a disadvantage in the questioning compared with the Australian community generally (s 23H(8))`
              ELSE IF `I have expressly and voluntarily waived an interview friend`
                   THEN `no: I have expressly and voluntarily waived my right to an interview friend; the prosecution must prove I did so with full knowledge and understanding (s 23H(2)(d), (4))`
                   ELSE `yes, unless I waive it: I am believed to be an Aboriginal person or Torres Strait Islander, so I must not be questioned unless an interview friend I choose (or, failing that, an Aboriginal legal assistance representative) is present and I have spoken with them privately; and unless I have arranged a lawyer, an Aboriginal legal assistance organisation must be notified first (s 23H)`

-- s 23N: "Where an investigating official believes on reasonable grounds that a person who is under arrest or a protected
-- suspect is unable, because of inadequate knowledge of the English language or a physical disability, to communicate orally
-- with reasonable fluency in that language, the official must, before starting to question the person, arrange for the
-- presence of an interpreter and defer the questioning". Not subject to s 23L.
@export I do not speak English well (or have a disability affecting speech). Must the police get me an interpreter before questioning?
GIVEN `I am under arrest or a protected suspect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under arrest for a Commonwealth offence, or a protected suspect (see the question on that)
      `the official believes I cannot communicate orally in English with reasonable fluency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official believes on reasonable grounds that, because of inadequate English or a physical disability, you cannot communicate orally in English with reasonable fluency
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the police get me an interpreter` IF
        `I am under arrest or a protected suspect`
    AND `the official believes I cannot communicate orally in English with reasonable fluency`

-- s 23P(1): "if a person who is under arrest or a protected suspect is not an Australian citizen", the official must "as soon as
-- practicable" tell me my consular office will be notified on request, notify it if I ask, tell me I may communicate with it,
-- give me facilities and a reasonable time; (2): questioning must not start until (1)(c), (d) and (f) are complied with
-- (subject to s 23L).
@export I am not an Australian citizen. Must the police let me contact my consulate before questioning?
GIVEN `I am under arrest or a protected suspect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under arrest for a Commonwealth offence, or a protected suspect
      `I am an Australian citizen` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an Australian citizen
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the police let me contact my consulate` IF
        `I am under arrest or a protected suspect`
    AND (NOT `I am an Australian citizen`)

§§ `s 23V -- if I made an admission to the police`

-- s 23V(1): "If a person who is being questioned as a suspect (whether under arrest or not) makes a confession or admission to an
-- investigating official, the confession or admission is inadmissible as evidence against the person in proceedings for any
-- Commonwealth offence unless: (a) if ... it was reasonably practicable to tape record the confession or admission--the
-- questioning of the person and anything said by the person during that questioning was tape recorded; or (b) in any other
-- case": a written record made at the time or as soon as practicable, read back to me in my language with a copy, my chance to
-- point out errors, a recording of the reading and my response, and the Schedule explanation -- asked as one fact.
-- (5): "A court may admit evidence ... even if the requirements ... have not been complied with ... if ... the court is
-- satisfied that, in the special circumstances of the case, admission of the evidence would not be contrary to the interests of
-- justice"; (7) the jury must then be told and warned. (2): I must get the recording, or a copy, within 7 days, free.
DECLARE `Can my unrecorded admission be used` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the questioning and what I said were tape recorded, and I must be given the recording or a copy within 7 days, free (s 23V(1)(a), (2))`
    `yes: recording was not reasonably practicable, and the written record was made, read back to me, copied to me, and the reading and my response recorded (s 23V(1)(b))`
    `only because the court is satisfied that, in the special circumstances, admitting it is not contrary to the interests of justice; the jury must be told of the non-compliance and warned (s 23V(5), (7))`
    `no: it is inadmissible against me, because it was not recorded as s 23V(1) requires and the court is not satisfied that admitting it would not be contrary to the interests of justice`

@export I made an admission to the police that was not recorded. Can it be used against me?
GIVEN `recording was reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was reasonably practicable to make an audio or video recording of the admission
      `the questioning and my answers were recorded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the questioning and everything you said during it were audio or video recorded
      `the written-record procedure was followed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether (where recording was not practicable) a written record was made at the time or soon after, read back to you in your language with a copy given to you, you were given the chance to point out errors, the reading and your response were recorded, and the procedure was explained to you first
      `the court is satisfied admitting it is not against the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that, in the special circumstances of the case, admitting the evidence despite the non-compliance would not be contrary to the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Can my unrecorded admission be used`
`Can my unrecorded admission be used question` MEANS
    IF `recording was reasonably practicable` AND `the questioning and my answers were recorded`
    THEN `yes: the questioning and what I said were tape recorded, and I must be given the recording or a copy within 7 days, free (s 23V(1)(a), (2))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `recording was reasonably practicable`) AND (`the questioning and my answers were recorded` OR `the written-record procedure was followed`)
         THEN `yes: recording was not reasonably practicable, and the written record was made, read back to me, copied to me, and the reading and my response recorded (s 23V(1)(b))`
         ELSE IF `the court is satisfied admitting it is not against the interests of justice`
              THEN `only because the court is satisfied that, in the special circumstances, admitting it is not contrary to the interests of justice; the jury must be told of the non-compliance and warned (s 23V(5), (7))`
              ELSE `no: it is inadmissible against me, because it was not recorded as s 23V(1) requires and the court is not satisfied that admitting it would not be contrary to the interests of justice`

-- s 23Q: "A person who is under arrest or a protected suspect must be treated with humanity and with respect for human dignity,
-- and must not be subjected to cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment."
DECLARE `How must I be treated in custody` IS ONE OF
    `with humanity and with respect for human dignity, and never cruelly, inhumanly or degradingly (s 23Q)`

@export How must the police treat me while I am under arrest or a protected suspect?
GIVETH A `How must I be treated in custody`
`How must I be treated in custody question` MEANS
    `with humanity and with respect for human dignity, and never cruelly, inhumanly or degradingly (s 23Q)`

§§ `ss 16A, 16C, 17A, 17B -- if you are being sentenced for a federal offence`

-- s 16A(1): "a court must impose a sentence or make an order that is of a severity appropriate in all the circumstances of the
-- offence." (2): "In addition to any other matters, the court must take into account such of the following matters as are
-- relevant and known to the court" -- the list (a)-(p) below. (2A): "the court must not take into account under subsection (1)
-- or (2), other than paragraph (2)(ma), any form of customary law or cultural practice as a reason for" lessening or
-- aggravating the seriousness, except for the heritage offences in (2AA). (2)(mb) racial or ethnic hatred aggravates, except for
-- the Criminal Code Part 5.1 urging-violence and hate offences listed in (2AAB).
DECLARE `A matter about me or my offence` IS ONE OF
    `the nature and circumstances of the offence`
    `other offences to be taken into account`
    `a course of conduct of similar criminal acts`
    `the victim's personal circumstances`
    `injury, loss or damage resulting from the offence`
    `a victim impact statement`
    `my contrition, or reparation I have made`
    `my failure to comply with pre-trial disclosure obligations`
    `my guilty plea and its timing`
    `my cooperation with law enforcement`
    `deterring me`
    `deterring others`
    `adequate punishment`
    `my character, antecedents, age, means and physical or mental condition`
    `that I used my standing in the community to commit the offence`
    `that the offence was motivated by racial or ethnic hatred`
    `my prospects of rehabilitation`
    `the effect of the sentence on my family or dependants`
    `customary law or cultural practice as lessening or aggravating the offence`

DECLARE `Must the court weigh this when sentencing me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must take it into account, so far as it is relevant and known to the court (s 16A(2))`
    `yes, as aggravating the offence, so far as it is relevant and known to the court (s 16A(2)(ma) or (mb))`
    `no: for this offence, racial or ethnic hatred is not taken into account as aggravating (s 16A(2AAB)); it is part of the offence itself`
    `no: the court must not take customary law or cultural practice into account as a reason to lessen or aggravate the seriousness of the offence (s 16A(2A))`
    `it may: for this heritage offence the bar on customary law and cultural practice does not apply (s 16A(2AA))`

@export Must the court take this into account when sentencing me for a federal offence?
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `A matter about me or my offence` @desc The matter you are asking about
      `my offence is a listed heritage offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your offence is one listed in s 16A(2AA) (cultural heritage offences under the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Heritage Protection Act 1984, listed EPBC Act provisions, and the others listed)
      `my offence is a listed Criminal Code hate offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your offence is one of the Criminal Code offences listed in s 16A(2AAB) (urging violence and related offences in ss 80.2A to 80.2DB)
GIVETH A `Must the court weigh this when sentencing me`
`Must the court weigh this when sentencing me question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `customary law or cultural practice as lessening or aggravating the offence` THEN
        IF `my offence is a listed heritage offence`
        THEN `it may: for this heritage offence the bar on customary law and cultural practice does not apply (s 16A(2AA))`
        ELSE `no: the court must not take customary law or cultural practice into account as a reason to lessen or aggravate the seriousness of the offence (s 16A(2A))`
    WHEN `that the offence was motivated by racial or ethnic hatred` THEN
        IF `my offence is a listed Criminal Code hate offence`
        THEN `no: for this offence, racial or ethnic hatred is not taken into account as aggravating (s 16A(2AAB)); it is part of the offence itself`
        ELSE `yes, as aggravating the offence, so far as it is relevant and known to the court (s 16A(2)(ma) or (mb))`
    WHEN `that I used my standing in the community to commit the offence` THEN `yes, as aggravating the offence, so far as it is relevant and known to the court (s 16A(2)(ma) or (mb))`
    OTHERWISE `yes: the court must take it into account, so far as it is relevant and known to the court (s 16A(2))`

-- s 16C(1): "before imposing a fine on a person for a federal offence, a court must take into account the financial
-- circumstances of the person"; (2): it may still fine me if my circumstances "cannot be ascertained".
@export Must the court consider what I can afford before fining me?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the court consider my finances before fining me` IF TRUE

-- s 17A(1): "A court shall not pass a sentence of imprisonment on any person for a federal offence ... unless the court, after
-- having considered all other available sentences, is satisfied that no other sentence is appropriate in all the circumstances
-- of the case." (2): it must state and record its reasons; (3): failure does not invalidate the sentence; (4): subject to a
-- contrary intention in the law creating the offence (a mandatory minimum, asked as one fact).
-- s 17B(1): if I am "convicted of one or more section 17B offences relating to property, money or both, whose total value is not
-- more than $2,000; and ... [I have] not previously been sentenced to imprisonment for any federal, State or Territory
-- offence", no imprisonment "unless the court is satisfied that there are exceptional circumstances". (3): "section 17B
-- offence" is Crimes Act s 29 or Criminal Code s 131.1 (theft), 132.1 (receiving), 132.6, 132.7, 134.1, 134.2, 135.1, 135.2,
-- 135.4 (fraud and dishonesty), 145.4 or 145.5, or a prescribed provision.
DECLARE `Can I be sent to prison` IS ONE OF
    `the law creating my offence requires or permits imprisonment despite s 17A (s 17A(4)); the rest of this answer does not apply`
    `no: for a minor property offence of $2,000 or less with no earlier prison sentence, prison is barred unless the court is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances, and it is not (s 17B(1))`
    `no: the court may imprison me only if, having considered all other available sentences, it is satisfied no other sentence is appropriate, and it is not so satisfied (s 17A(1))`
    `yes, as a last resort: the court is satisfied that no other sentence is appropriate, and must state and record its reasons (s 17A(1), (2))`

@export If I am convicted of a federal offence, can I be sent to prison?
GIVEN `the law creating my offence overrides s 17A` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law creating your offence shows a contrary intention to s 17A, for example a mandatory minimum term of imprisonment
      `it is a section 17B property or money offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each offence you are convicted of is a section 17B offence relating to property or money (Crimes Act s 29, or Criminal Code s 131.1, 132.1, 132.6, 132.7, 134.1, 134.2, 135.1, 135.2, 135.4, 145.4 or 145.5, or a prescribed provision)
      `the total value in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value of the property or money involved in all of those offences, including any the court takes into account with your consent
      `I have been sentenced to imprisonment before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have ever before been sentenced to imprisonment for any federal, State or Territory offence
      `the court is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that there are exceptional circumstances warranting imprisonment
      `the court is satisfied no other sentence is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, after considering all other available sentences, is satisfied that no sentence other than imprisonment is appropriate in all the circumstances
GIVETH A `Can I be sent to prison`
`Can I be sent to prison question` MEANS
    IF `the law creating my offence overrides s 17A`
    THEN `the law creating my offence requires or permits imprisonment despite s 17A (s 17A(4)); the rest of this answer does not apply`
    ELSE IF     `it is a section 17B property or money offence`
                 AND `the total value in dollars` AT MOST 2000
                 AND (NOT `I have been sentenced to imprisonment before`)
                 AND (NOT `the court is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances`)
         THEN `no: for a minor property offence of $2,000 or less with no earlier prison sentence, prison is barred unless the court is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances, and it is not (s 17B(1))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the court is satisfied no other sentence is appropriate`)
              THEN `no: the court may imprison me only if, having considered all other available sentences, it is satisfied no other sentence is appropriate, and it is not so satisfied (s 17A(1))`
              ELSE `yes, as a last resort: the court is satisfied that no other sentence is appropriate, and must state and record its reasons (s 17A(1), (2))`

§§ `ss 19B, 20 -- avoiding a conviction, or release on a recognizance (if the charge is proved)`

-- s 19B(1): where "the court is satisfied ... that the charge is proved, but is of the opinion, having regard to: (i) the
-- character, antecedents, age, health or mental condition of the person; (ii) the extent (if any) to which the offence is of a
-- trivial nature; or (iii) the extent (if any) to which the offence was committed under extenuating circumstances; that it is
-- inexpedient to inflict any punishment, or to inflict any punishment other than a nominal punishment, or that it is expedient
-- to release the offender on probation; the court may, by order: (c) dismiss the charge ...; or (d) discharge the person,
-- without proceeding to conviction ..., upon his or her giving security ... by recognizance ... that he or she will comply
-- with the following conditions: (i) ... good behaviour for such period, not exceeding 3 years ...; (ii) ... reparation,
-- restitution, ... compensation ... costs ...; (iii) ... during a period, not exceeding 2 years, ... such other conditions".
-- (2A): no imprisonment "for a failure to pay an amount required to be paid under an order made under this section". The
-- court's opinion and its choice are inputs.
DECLARE `Can I avoid a conviction` IS ONE OF
    `no: the court is not of the opinion s 19B requires, so it cannot dismiss the charge or discharge me without conviction under s 19B`
    `yes: the court may dismiss the charge without convicting me (s 19B(1)(c))`
    `yes: the court may discharge me without conviction on a recognizance to be of good behaviour for up to 3 years, with reparation, compensation or costs and up to 2 years of other conditions, such as supervision (s 19B(1)(d)); I cannot be imprisoned for failing to pay under it (s 19B(2A))`

@export The charge against me has been proved. Can the court let me go without recording a conviction?
GIVEN `the court is of the s 19B opinion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, having regard to your character, antecedents, age, health or mental condition, how trivial the offence is, or any extenuating circumstances, is of the opinion that it is inexpedient to punish you (or to punish you more than nominally), or that it is expedient to release you on probation
      `the court chooses to dismiss the charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court chooses to dismiss the charge outright (TRUE) rather than discharge you on a recognizance with conditions (FALSE)
GIVETH A `Can I avoid a conviction`
`Can I avoid a conviction question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the court is of the s 19B opinion`)
    THEN `no: the court is not of the opinion s 19B requires, so it cannot dismiss the charge or discharge me without conviction under s 19B`
    ELSE IF `the court chooses to dismiss the charge`
         THEN `yes: the court may dismiss the charge without convicting me (s 19B(1)(c))`
         ELSE `yes: the court may discharge me without conviction on a recognizance to be of good behaviour for up to 3 years, with reparation, compensation or costs and up to 2 years of other conditions, such as supervision (s 19B(1)(d)); I cannot be imprisoned for failing to pay under it (s 19B(2A))`

-- s 20(1)(a): after conviction the court may "release the person, without passing sentence ..., upon his or her giving security
-- ... that he or she will comply with ... (i) ... good behaviour for such period, not exceeding 5 years ...; (ii) reparation ...
-- (iii) ... pay to the Commonwealth such pecuniary penalty (if any) as the court specifies ... (iv) ... during a period, not
-- exceeding 2 years, ... such other conditions". (5): "The maximum amount of the penalty ... is: (a) where the offence is
-- punishable by a fine--the amount of the maximum fine that the court is empowered to impose on the person for the offence; or
-- (b) where the offence is not punishable by a fine: (i) if the court is not a court of summary jurisdiction--300 penalty
-- units; or (ii) if the court is a court of summary jurisdiction--60 penalty units." The answer is in penalty units; their
-- dollar value is not computed (finding 14).
@export If I am released on a s 20 recognizance without being sentenced, what is the most the court can make me pay as a penalty, in penalty units?
GIVEN `my offence is punishable by a fine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence you were convicted of is punishable by a fine
      `the maximum fine in penalty units` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum fine, in penalty units, the court could impose on you for the offence (ignored if it is not punishable by a fine)
      `I am in a court of summary jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court that convicted you is a court of summary jurisdiction (a local or magistrates court), rather than a higher court
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most a s 20 recognizance can make me pay in penalty units` MEANS
    IF `my offence is punishable by a fine`
    THEN `the maximum fine in penalty units`
    ELSE IF `I am in a court of summary jurisdiction`
         THEN 60
         ELSE 300

-- s 20(1)(a)(i) and (iv): the periods.
@export If I am released on a s 20 recognizance, for how long can I be bound to be of good behaviour, in years?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The longest good behaviour period under a s 20 recognizance in years` MEANS 5

@export If I am discharged without conviction under s 19B, for how long can I be bound to be of good behaviour, in years?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The longest good behaviour period under a s 19B discharge in years` MEANS 3

§§ `Part VIIC -- when my conviction is spent (if you have a conviction)`

-- s 85ZM(1): "a person shall be taken to have been convicted of an offence if: (a) the person has been convicted ...; (b) the
-- person has been charged with, and found guilty of, the offence but discharged without conviction; or (c) ... a court has
-- taken it into account in passing sentence ... for another offence." (2): "a person's conviction of an offence is spent if:
-- (a) the person has been granted a pardon for a reason other than that the person was wrongly convicted ...; or (b) the
-- person was not sentenced to imprisonment for the offence, or was not sentenced to imprisonment for the offence for more than 30
-- months, and the waiting period for the offence has ended." s 85ZL: "waiting period ... means: (a) if the person ... was dealt
-- with as a minor ...--the period of 5 years beginning on the day on which the person was convicted ...; or (b) in any other
-- case--the period of 10 years beginning on the day on which the person was convicted". The period "beginning on" the day of
-- conviction ends at the end of the day before the anniversary; the conviction is spent from the anniversary.
-- s 85ZX(2), 85ZY: a further conviction "on indictment" (by a federal or Territory court) or by a State or foreign court, "of
-- another offence, being an offence committed during that waiting period", stops Division 3 applying "until the waiting
-- period for the later offence has ended"; (1): a summary conviction by a federal or Territory court does so only if "the
-- court ... order[s]" it. "Dealt with as a minor" is not defined in Part VIIC: asked as a fact.
GIVEN `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE
      `I was dealt with as a minor` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the day my waiting period ends after` MEANS
    IF `I was dealt with as a minor`
    THEN `add months` `the day of the conviction` 60
    ELSE `add months` `the day of the conviction` 120

GIVEN `the day asked about` IS A DATE
      `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE
      `I was dealt with as a minor` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a later offence in the waiting period stops it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the later conviction` IS A DATE
      `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my conviction is spent on the day` IF
        `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction`
    OR  (    ((NOT `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it`) OR `the months of imprisonment` AT MOST 30)
         AND `the day asked about` AT LEAST (`the day my waiting period ends after` `the day of the conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor`)
         AND (    (NOT `a later offence in the waiting period stops it`)
              OR  `the day asked about` AT LEAST (`the day my waiting period ends after` `the day of the later conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence`)))

@export Is my conviction spent on a given day?
GIVEN `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about
      `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been granted a pardon for the offence for a reason other than that you were wrongly convicted
      `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were sentenced to imprisonment for the offence (including a suspended sentence or one served by intensive correction)
      `the months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER @desc The length of that sentence of imprisonment, in months (0 if none)
      `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE @desc The day you were convicted (or found guilty and discharged without conviction, or the day the court took the offence into account)
      `I was dealt with as a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dealt with as a minor (a juvenile) for the conviction
      `a later offence in the waiting period stops it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were later convicted of another offence that you committed during the waiting period, and that conviction was on indictment, or by a State or foreign court, or the court convicting you summarily ordered that the earlier conviction not become spent
      `the day of the later conviction` IS A DATE @desc The day of that later conviction (ignored if there is none)
      `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dealt with as a minor for the later conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my conviction spent` IF
    `my conviction is spent on the day` `the day asked about` `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction` `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it` `the months of imprisonment` `the day of the conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor` `a later offence in the waiting period stops it` `the day of the later conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence`

@export If my conviction can become spent, on what day does that happen (absent a later offence)?
GIVEN `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE @desc The day you were convicted (or found guilty and discharged without conviction)
      `I was dealt with as a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dealt with as a minor (a juvenile) for the conviction
GIVETH A DATE
`The first day my conviction is spent` MEANS
    `the day my waiting period ends after` `the day of the conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor`

-- s 85ZV(1): "if a person's conviction of a Commonwealth offence or a Territory offence is spent, the person is not required: (a)
-- in any State or Territory--to disclose to any person, for any purpose, the fact that the person has been charged with, or
-- convicted of, the offence". (2): for "a State offence or a foreign offence", only "in any Territory" or to "any Commonwealth
-- authority". s 85ZW(a): "it is lawful for the person to claim ... on oath or otherwise, that he or she was not charged with,
-- or convicted of, the offence"; (b) others who know must not disclose or take it into account.
-- Division 6 exclusions: work with children, with people with disability and in funded aged care, for prescribed screening
-- bodies (ss 85ZZGB-85ZZGD and the matching Subdivisions; the bodies are prescribed by regulation, not deposited); s 85ZZH:
-- (a) a law enforcement agency deciding on prosecution or sentencing or assessing prospective employees; (b) an intelligence or
-- security agency assessing staff; (c) "a court or tribunal ... for the purpose of making a decision, including a decision in
-- relation to sentencing"; (d) a decision-maker under the Migration Act 1958 or the Australian Citizenship Act 2007; (g) a
-- Commonwealth authority filling a designated security position; (h) AUSTRAC staff; (j) the Australian Government Solicitor
-- prosecuting; (k) a prescribed person for a prescribed offence (not deposited).
DECLARE `Who is asking about my conviction` IS ONE OF
    `an employer, landlord, insurer or anyone else for an ordinary purpose`
    `a Commonwealth authority, for an ordinary purpose`
    `a screening body for work with children, people with disability or aged care`
    `a police force or prosecutor deciding on prosecution or sentencing, or assessing me for a job with it`
    `an intelligence or security agency assessing me for a job, or a Commonwealth authority for a designated security position`
    `a court or tribunal making a decision`
    `a decision-maker under the Migration Act or the Citizenship Act`

DECLARE `Must I disclose my conviction` IS ONE OF
    `no: my conviction is spent, so I need not disclose that I was charged with or convicted of the offence, I may lawfully say I was not, and anyone who knows must not disclose it or take it into account without my consent (ss 85ZV, 85ZW)`
    `yes, if asked: my conviction is not spent, so Part VIIC gives me no right not to disclose it`
    `yes, if asked: although my conviction is spent, Part VIIC Division 6 lets this person or body be told and take it into account for this purpose (ss 85ZZGB-85ZZGD, 85ZZH)`

@export Must I disclose this conviction when asked?
GIVEN `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are being asked
      `it is a State offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conviction is for an offence against a State law (including the ACT and NT, which Part VIIC counts as States), rather than a Commonwealth or external Territory offence
      `the person asking` IS A `Who is asking about my conviction` @desc Who is asking, and for what purpose
      `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been granted a pardon for the offence for a reason other than that you were wrongly convicted
      `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were sentenced to imprisonment for the offence
      `the months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER @desc The length of that sentence of imprisonment, in months (0 if none)
      `the day of the conviction` IS A DATE @desc The day you were convicted (or found guilty and discharged without conviction)
      `I was dealt with as a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dealt with as a minor (a juvenile) for the conviction
      `a later offence in the waiting period stops it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were later convicted of another offence committed during the waiting period, on indictment or by a State or foreign court, or the summary court ordered that the earlier conviction not become spent
      `the day of the later conviction` IS A DATE @desc The day of that later conviction (ignored if there is none)
      `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dealt with as a minor for the later conviction
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Must I disclose my conviction`
`Must I disclose this conviction` MEANS
    IF `it is a State offence` AND (NOT (`the person asking` EQUALS `a Commonwealth authority, for an ordinary purpose`))
    THEN `not covered` `my conviction is for a State offence and the person asking is not a Commonwealth authority in a State: the State's own spent convictions law decides it (s 85ZV(2), (3)), and that law is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`my conviction is spent on the day` `the day asked about` `I was pardoned for a reason other than wrongful conviction` `I was sentenced to imprisonment for it` `the months of imprisonment` `the day of the conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor` `a later offence in the waiting period stops it` `the day of the later conviction` `I was dealt with as a minor for the later offence`))
         THEN `answered` `yes, if asked: my conviction is not spent, so Part VIIC gives me no right not to disclose it`
         ELSE IF (`the person asking` EQUALS `an employer, landlord, insurer or anyone else for an ordinary purpose`) OR (`the person asking` EQUALS `a Commonwealth authority, for an ordinary purpose`)
              THEN `answered` `no: my conviction is spent, so I need not disclose that I was charged with or convicted of the offence, I may lawfully say I was not, and anyone who knows must not disclose it or take it into account without my consent (ss 85ZV, 85ZW)`
              ELSE `answered` `yes, if asked: although my conviction is spent, Part VIIC Division 6 lets this person or body be told and take it into account for this purpose (ss 85ZZGB-85ZZGD, 85ZZH)`
